WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:01.580
This creature was crowned

00:00:01.580 --> 00:00:04.090
the world's ugliest animal in 2013,

00:00:04.090 --> 00:00:06.860
a title it still defends today.

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:11.670
On land, he's got a body like
Jell-O and a big old frown.

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:15.450
But drop this fellow 9,200
feet below sea level,

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:19.230
and the water holds up all
that flab like a push-up bra,

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:21.860
making the fish a little more handsome.

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:25.160
Same old fish, but with
a little more support.

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:28.247
So, what is all that water
pressure holding together?

00:00:28.247 --> 00:00:31.744
David Stein: Between the
skin, that flabby skin,

00:00:31.744 --> 00:00:35.260
and the muscles is a lot of fluid.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:38.680
Narrator: This is David Stein,
a deep-sea-fish biologist

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:43.290
who was lucky enough to dissect
19 blobfishes in the 1970s.

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:46.950
Blobfish look blobby because
they are full of water.

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:49.440
Under their skin, blobfish
have a thick layer

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:52.985
of gelatinous flesh that
floats outside their muscles.

00:00:52.985 --> 00:00:55.363
Stein: If you pick up
a blobfish by the tail,

00:00:55.363 --> 00:00:59.650
then it kind of flows to the head.

00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:01.910
Narrator: This water-filled,
Jell-O-like layer

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:04.950
allows the blobfish to
stay somewhat buoyant,

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:07.200
which is important because blobfishes

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.091
don't have a swim bladder.

00:01:09.091 --> 00:01:11.160
Stein: And fishes that have swim bladders

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:14.900
are able to adjust their buoyancy.

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:18.600
They can secrete gas into the swim bladder

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:20.008
or remove it.

00:01:20.008 --> 00:01:22.167
A fish that lives on the bottom

00:01:22.167 --> 00:01:25.365
doesn't need to be able
to maintain its buoyancy.

00:01:25.365 --> 00:01:28.360
Narrator: So, the Jell-O layer
isn't a perfect substitute,

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:31.460
but the blobfish doesn't
need to be a strong swimmer.

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:34.410
The predator has a highly
specialized hunting strategy

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:37.300
that's perfect for the rocky
barrens of the deep sea.

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:39.900
Stein: It just sits there and
waits for dinner to come by.

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:41.210
Narrator: If all you do is sit,

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:43.090
you don't need much under your skin.

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:46.023
Just watery tissue, some
yellow pockets of fat,

00:01:46.023 --> 00:01:47.740
and a smidgen of muscle.

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:49.020
In case you hadn't guessed,

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:51.110
blobfishes aren't exactly yoked.

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:52.840
They have very little red muscle,

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:55.550
the kind that allows you,
a human, to run a mile

00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:58.350
or a tuna fish to migrate across oceans.

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:01.020
Instead, blobfish have
a lot of white muscle,

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:03.058
which allows them to swim in short bursts

00:02:03.058 --> 00:02:06.170
and lunge at prey that
on occasion ramble by.

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:08.150
This is a baby blobfish.

00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:10.250
It's a cleared and stained specimen,

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:12.330
meaning all its tissue has been dissolved

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:14.720
to show only the bones and cartilage.

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:16.670
Those thin red lines you see,

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:19.610
they're the blobfish's bones dyed red.

00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:21.310
If you're having trouble seeing the bones,

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:22.830
you're not the only one.

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:25.280
Blobfish have poorly ossified skeletons,

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:26.870
meaning they're thinner and more fragile

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:29.500
than the bones of most shallow-water fish.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:32.030
This is another handy deep-sea adaptation,

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:35.350
as it takes a lot of precious
energy to build strong bones.

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:37.323
But the blobfish saves its energy

00:02:37.323 --> 00:02:39.740
to develop what might be
the most important bone

00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:41.890
in its body: its jaws,

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:45.080
which also happened to be the
reason it looks so gloomy.

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:48.450
The fish needs enormous jaws
so it can snap up any prey

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:50.840
that passes by and swallow it whole,

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:54.450
maybe even smacking its
blubbery lips as it eats.

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:56.600
And that brings us to its stomach.

00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:58.740
If you're the kind of
creature that eats anything

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:01.090
that swims by, some surprising things

00:03:01.090 --> 00:03:02.700
can wind up in your stomach.

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:04.940
Stein found a wide range of foods

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:08.250
and not-foods in the
blobfish he dissected.

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:13.040
Fish, sea pens, brittle stars,
hermit crabs, an anemone,

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:16.170
a plastic bag, and also lots of rocks.

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:18.670
Stein: Their stomach contents
kind of bear out the fact

00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:21.840
that they're probably not too bright.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:23.780
Narrator: He also found octopus beaks,

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:26.670
the cephalopods' hard, indigestible jaws.

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:29.140
This means that one of the
world's flabbiest fishes

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:30.617
has been able to eat one of the sea's

00:03:30.617 --> 00:03:32.120
most cunning predators.

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:33.620
If you're surprised, just think

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:35.650
about the blobfish's thick skin.

00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:37.750
What would it be harder
to grab in a fight:

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:40.140
a sack of bones or a sack of Jell-O?

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:42.269
Stein suspects it might be the latter.

00:03:42.269 --> 00:03:44.913
Stein: If the skin is
loose, perhaps the suckers

00:03:44.913 --> 00:03:47.290
can't really get a good grip on it.

00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:48.760
Narrator: Stein found sucker marks

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:50.435
across the blobfish's body,

00:03:50.435 --> 00:03:52.026
a hint that the fish might've been

00:03:52.026 --> 00:03:53.851
in some deep-sea fights.

00:03:53.851 --> 00:03:55.500
So while all of this Jell-O might look

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:57.872
a little unconventional, well,

00:03:57.872 --> 00:04:00.230
it seems to have served its purpose.

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:03.280
The blobfish is perfectly
suited to life in the deep sea,

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:06.200
where beauty standards are
probably quite different.

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:07.033
After all...

00:04:07.033 --> 00:04:09.433
Stein: Ugly is kind of in
the eye of the beholder.

